---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Runtime"
  description="Understand the separate mdbase collection, runtime-contract, workflow-execution, and Connect hosting layers."
  current="runtime"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero__inner">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow"><i></i> Runtime profile 0.1</p>
        <h1>Runtime contracts and workflow execution.</h1>
      </div>
      <div class="page-hero__aside">
        <p>
          mdbase separates passive collection semantics from declared provider
          contracts and durable workflow execution. Hosts declare support for
          each layer independently.
        </p>
        <div class="button-row">
          <a class="button button--primary" href="/spec/#section-13">Runtime specification</a>
          <a class="button" href="/implementations/">Implementation profiles</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2>Four related layers</h2>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step">
        <div>
          <h3>Collection semantics</h3>
          <p>
            Loading, typing, validation, CEL queries, links, saved views,
            revision-safe mutations, lifecycle policy, and watching. These
            behaviors operate directly on the collection.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div>
          <h3>Runtime Contracts 0.1</h3>
          <p>
            Portable event and action declarations, provider registration,
            compatibility checks, and preflight. The TypeScript package
            implements this browser-safe provider-host layer.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div>
          <h3>Workflow execution 0.1</h3>
          <p>
            Durable journals, idempotent actions, timers, recovery, and
            provider-neutral execution. The Rust <code>mdbase-runtime</code>{" "}
            crate implements this execution layer with in-memory, SQLite, and
            PostgreSQL storage.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step">
        <div>
          <h3>Connect embedding</h3>
          <p>
            Connect compiles exact application grants into runtime behavior,
            supplies local or hosted collection authorities, and routes
            notification signals. The runtime profile defines the contracts
            that Connect hosts.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="content-section">
    <h2>Conformance profiles</h2>
    <p>
      Conformance uses atomic profiles. Provider hosts claim{" "}
      <code>runtime_contracts/0.1</code>. Workflow engines add{" "}
      <code>workflow/0.1</code> after publishing shared fixture evidence.
    </p>
    <table class="profile-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Profile</th>
          <th>Covered behavior</th>
          <th>Separate profiles</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>runtime_contracts/0.1</code></td>
          <td>Declaration validation, registry behavior, compatibility, dispatch, preflight</td>
          <td>Watching, timers, durable execution, or recovery</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>workflow/0.1</code></td>
          <td>Durable workflow execution, action idempotency, timers, and recovery</td>
          <td>All collection semantics unless separately claimed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>watch</code></td>
          <td>Real collection event delivery and ordering</td>
          <td>Runtime provider or workflow behavior</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
